# <<.Name>>, for an agent

Read `<<.Name>> describe --json` first. It is the whole surface — every command,
its flags and arguments, the screens, the palette and the glyph set — in one
call, generated from the same declaration the tool runs on. It cannot describe a
flag the tool does not have.

## Where things live

- `internal/engine` — the domain. Change what <<.Name>> knows about here.
  It must not import anything terminal-shaped; a test enforces that.
- `internal/tui` — the interface. Regions are named in `regions.go`; a click
  resolves to a region name and index, never a coordinate.
- `internal/cli` — the command tree. Adding a command here adds it to the CLI,
  to `describe --json`, and (given a `Target`) to a context menu. Do not add it
  in more than one place; there is only one place.

### Where <<.Name>>'s own files go

`internal/engine/paths.go`. Config is `~/.config/<<.Name>>/` (or
`$XDG_CONFIG_HOME`); state is `~/.local/state/<<.Name>>/` (or `$XDG_STATE_HOME`)
— on every platform, macOS included.

**Do not replace this with `os.UserConfigDir()`.** It ignores XDG on darwin and
answers `~/Library/Application Support`, which is not where a developer's tools
live and not a directory anybody syncs. `paths_test.go` fails if you do, and it
names both paths when it does. tuikit decision 33.

Config is written by a person and belongs in a dotfiles repository; state is
written by <<.Name>> and must not follow anyone to another machine. Keep them
apart.

That file is copied into <<.Name>> rather than imported from tuikit, because
config is engine work and the engine imports no tuikit. It is yours — change it.

## Looking at the screens

You cannot see a terminal, but you can read one. Every screen has a golden in
`internal/tui/testdata` — a captured frame with the color stripped — so
"what does this look like at 80 columns" is a file, not a question for a human.

```sh
<<.Name>> browse --snapshot /tmp/frames --script 'press j j; shot moved; click items.row[2]; shot picked'
make frames     # or: render every captured screen to an HTML page
```

`--snapshot` is the one that needs no test: it drives the real binary with keys
and clicks addressed by REGION NAME, and `shot <name>` says where the
interesting frames are. `--help` and `describe --json` both list it, so you do
not have to be told it exists.

The goldens are rendered from `fixture()` in `internal/tui/fixture_test.go`,
not from a live backend. To see a state that does not exist yet, add it to
`states()` in `capture_test.go` and rerun with `-update-goldens`; the list is
walked by the goldens, the 80-column run, the color check and the capture, so
one entry gets you all four.

## Before you say you are done

```
make check
```

That is what CI runs. If a screen changed on purpose, `go test ./internal/tui -update-goldens`
and read the diff in the goldens — they are plain text, and a surprise in one is
the point of having them.

## Rules that will fail your change if you break them

- A color must come from `Palette`, named by the ROLE it plays. A raw
  `lipgloss.Color("205")` fails, because it says what a color IS instead of
  what it is FOR.
- A non-ASCII character must be in `Glyphs`. Add it deliberately, with the
  reason, or use ASCII.
- The engine imports the standard library and its own backend's SDK. Nothing
  else.
- Anything a mouse can do, a key must also do.

## Keeping up with tuikit

tuikit is resolved through `replace => ../tuikit`, so it has no version: a pull
there changes <<.Name>>'s behavior with nothing to bump and nothing to read.
Two commands close that gap, and **both are worth running before you build any
interface**.

```sh
tuikit news           # what tuikit decided since <<.Name>> last looked
tuikit gallery -list  # everything comp already has, so you do not rebuild it
```

`news` needs no arguments from inside this repo — it finds tuikit through the
`go.mod` replace directive and reads the marker below. When you have read what
it prints, move the number.

Do not hand-write a list of tuikit features here; a generated one cannot go
stale and a written one always does.

Reconciled with tuikit through decision <<.Decision>>.

## When comp cannot do something

You will hit this. The rule is **not** to work around it quietly.

If you are about to write `c.Text`, `c.Fill`, `c.Set`, or arithmetic on a Rect,
stop: the canvas exists so a tool never does that, and a tool that had to is a
tool the framework failed. Work around it if you must — you have a thing to
ship — but **report it in the same commit**, or it will not be reported.

```sh
gh issue create -R richarddavenport/tuikit --template from-a-tool.md
```

The template asks for four things, and the second is the one that matters:

1. what the tool was doing
2. **the code you wrote by hand** — the actual code, not a description
3. which component you reached for and what stopped you
4. whether you computed a coordinate

Why the code: a component gets built when two tools have hand-rolled the SAME
thing, and "the same" is a judgement nobody can make from a description.
`app.Toggles` exists because two tools' versions turned out to be identical.
`comp.Tree` does *not* exist because two tools' versions turned out to differ in
a way that mattered — one carried a bucket and a resource, the other a service
and a change, and a shared node type would have made both of them worse.

A report with the code is evidence. A report without it is a feature request,
and tuikit does not take those.

**A guess at the API is welcome and a wrong guess is useful.** Say if you have
none.
